{:edb/db-before-t 9966 :edb/db-after-t 9967 :edb/tx-data [[13194139543279 50 #inst "2026-10-28T17:36:58.815Z" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186051608 1078 #edb/ref 17592186051609 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186051614 1078 #edb/ref 17592186051617 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186051632 1078 #edb/ref 17592186051636 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186052177 1078 #edb/ref 17592186052178 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186052183 1078 #edb/ref 17592186052186 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186052189 1078 #edb/ref 17592186052192 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186055013 1074 "Implicit differentiation gives $2x+8y\\,y'=0$, so $y'=-x/(4y)$ wherever $y\\ne0$. At $(\\sqrt2,1)$, the slope is $$-\\frac{\\sqrt2}{4}=-\\frac1{2\\sqrt2}.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186055014 1074 "Implicit differentiation gives the tangent slope $-x/(4y)=-1/(2\\sqrt2)$ at $(\\sqrt2,1)$. The normal slope is its negative reciprocal, $2\\sqrt2$. Thus the normal is $y-1=2\\sqrt2(x-\\sqrt2)$. Setting $y=0$ yields $$x=\\sqrt2-\\frac1{2\\sqrt2}=\\frac3{2\\sqrt2}.$$ Therefore $P=(\\tfrac3{2\\sqrt2},0)$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186055017 1074 "Differentiating gives $6x-4y\\,y'=0$, hence the tangent slope at $(2,2)$ is $3/2$. The normal slope is $-2/3$, so its equation is $y-2=-\\tfrac23(x-2)$. At $x=0$, $y=2+\\tfrac43=\\tfrac{10}3$. Thus $P=(0,\\tfrac{10}3)$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186055138 1074 "Differentiate implicitly: $3x^2+3y^2y'=0$. Hence $$\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x^2}{y^2}$$ wherever the curve has a finite derivative, namely $y\\ne0$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186055139 1074 "Differentiate implicitly: $$-\\sin y\\,y'+4x-y'=10.$$ Solving for the derivative gives $$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4x-10}{1+\\sin y}=\\frac{2(2x-5)}{\\sin y+1},$$ where the denominator is nonzero." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186055140 1074 "Apply the product and chain rules: $y+xy'+2yy'=6x$. Thus $$(x+2y)y'=6x-y\\implies\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{6x-y}{x+2y},$$ where $x+2y\\ne0$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186056113 1078 #edb/ref 17592186056114 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186056430 1078 #edb/ref 17592186056431 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186056532 1078 #edb/ref 17592186056535 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186057072 1078 #edb/ref 17592186057076 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186057114 1078 #edb/ref 17592186057118 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186057120 1078 #edb/ref 17592186057121 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186057126 1078 #edb/ref 17592186057131 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186057132 1078 #edb/ref 17592186057136 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186058149 1078 #edb/ref 17592186058151 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186058206 1078 #edb/ref 17592186058210 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186058224 1078 #edb/ref 17592186058226 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186058482 1078 #edb/ref 17592186058484 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186058488 1078 #edb/ref 17592186058492 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186059528 1074 "We have\n\n$$\nOA^(→) = a = i + 3j,; OB^(→) = b = 2i - 2j\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB^(→) &= OB^(→) - OA^(→) \\\\\n&= b - a \\\\\n&= (2i - 2j) - (i + 3j) \\\\\n&= i - 5j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid AB^(→) \\mid &= \\sqrt{1^{2} + (-5)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 25} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{26}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186059528 1074 "We have\n\n$$\\overset{\\to}{OA} = a = i + 3j,; \\overset{\\to}{OB} = b = 2i - 2j$$\n\nExpressing $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\overset{\\to}{AB} &= \\overset{\\to}{OB} - \\overset{\\to}{OA} \\\\\n&= b - a \\\\\n&= (2i - 2j) - (i + 3j) \\\\\n&= i - 5j\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\overset{\\to}{AB} \\mid &= \\sqrt{1^{2} + (-5)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 25} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{26}\n\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186059530 1074 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + 2b}{\\mid a + 2b \\mid}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + 2b &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + 2 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 + 2 \\\\ 0 + (-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a + 2b \\mid &= \\sqrt{0^{2} + (-2)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + 2b}{\\mid a + 2b \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(a + 2b) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186059530 1074 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}}{|\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}|}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the sum:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2+2 \\\\ 0+(-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a + 2b \\mid &= \\sqrt{0^{2} + (-2)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}}{|\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186059959 1074 "The strict inequalities exclude both endpoints. Use parentheses at both ends: $x\\in(3,7)$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060012 1074 "The lower endpoint $9$ is excluded, and the upper endpoint $13$ is included. Thus $x\\in(9,13]$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060029 1071 "If $\\mathbf{v}=⟨4,-1⟩$, then what is the magnitude of $\\mathbf{v}$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060029 1071 "If $v = ⟨4,-1⟩$, then what is the magnitude of $v$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186060029 1074 "Use the Euclidean norm: $$\\|v\\|=\\sqrt{4^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt{17}.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060119 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}$ and points the same direction." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060119 1071 "Let $a = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a + 3b$ and points the same direction." 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186060119 1074 "First $a+3b=\\langle-1,0\\rangle+3\\langle3,-2\\rangle=\\langle8,-6\\rangle$. Its norm is $10$. Dividing by this positive norm preserves direction and gives the unit vector $\\langle4/5,-3/5\\rangle$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060126 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-12,3⟩$. Find $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060126 1071 "Let $a = ⟨ - 12, 3⟩$. Find $\\mid a \\mid$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186060126 1074 "Use the Euclidean norm: $$\\|a\\|=\\sqrt{(-12)^2+3^2}=\\sqrt{153}=3\\sqrt{17}.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060127 1071 "![](images/2c/2c1f9580f07d06af7ec900ee63c95872e06f459107e64cf1b5117d928efdab22.png)\n\nLet $A(-4, 1)$ and $B(3, 0)$. Find $\\mid AB^(→) \\mid$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186060127 1071 "![](images/2c/2c1f9580f07d06af7ec900ee63c95872e06f459107e64cf1b5117d928efdab22.png)\n\nLet $A(-4, 1)$ and $B(3, 0)$. Find $\\mid \\overset{\\to}{AB} \\mid$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060127 1074 "The displacement is $\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\langle3-(-4),0-1\\rangle=\\langle7,-1\\rangle$. Its length is $$\\sqrt{7^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt{50}=5\\sqrt2.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060128 1071 "![](images/0d/0d92a4d7b9c59b0d6bc8ffec9926bcf189c9bbe808c5bed7a02d6ab09bcebcd0.png)\n\nLet $P(-2, 2)$ and $Q(3, 3)$. Find $\\mid PQ^(→) \\mid$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186060128 1071 "![](images/0d/0d92a4d7b9c59b0d6bc8ffec9926bcf189c9bbe808c5bed7a02d6ab09bcebcd0.png)\n\nLet $P(-2, 2)$ and $Q(3, 3)$. Find $\\mid \\overset{\\to}{PQ} \\mid$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060128 1074 "The displacement is $\\overrightarrow{PQ}=\\langle3-(-2),3-2\\rangle=\\langle5,1\\rangle$. Its length is $$\\sqrt{5^2+1^2}=\\sqrt{26}.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060129 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$ and points the same direction." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060129 1071 "Let $a = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a$ and points the same direction." 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186060129 1074 "The norm of $a$ is $\\sqrt{3^2+8^2}=\\sqrt{73}$. Dividing by this positive number preserves direction and gives $$\\frac{a}{\\|a\\|}=\\begin{bmatrix}3/\\sqrt{73}\\\\8/\\sqrt{73}\\end{bmatrix}.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060252 1074 "The square bracket includes $-5$ and the parenthesis excludes $2\\sqrt2$. Therefore $-5\\le y<2\\sqrt2$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060264 1074 "The open circle at $-1$ excludes that endpoint, and the filled circle at $2$ includes that endpoint. The segment represents $x\\in(-1,2]$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060267 1074 "Both endpoint circles, at $1$ and $4$, are open. The segment represents $x\\in(1,4)$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060310 1074 "Both parentheses exclude their endpoints. Therefore $x\\in(3,4)$ means $3<x<4$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060311 1074 "Both square brackets include their endpoints. Therefore $x\\in[-2,1]$ means $-2\\le x\\le1$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060910 1064 "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,4 \\rangle$ shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd458eeb41ee09d6a4f58bc343575d46e3d362dc3edca40a47e1a7381fcc9bd2.png)\n\nExpressed in component form, our vector is $\\mathbf{a}=-2\\mathbf{i} + 4 \\mathbf{j}.$\n\nTo find the magnitude (length) of $\\mathbf{a},$ we can use the Pythagorean theorem on $\\triangle OXA.$\n\nThe legs of the triangle have lengths\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{OX}| & =|-2|=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{XA}| & =|\\,4\\,|=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the Pythagorean theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{|\\overset{\\to }{OX}{|}^{2}+|\\overset{\\to }{XA}{|}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186060910 1064 "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,4 \\rangle$ shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd458eeb41ee09d6a4f58bc343575d46e3d362dc3edca40a47e1a7381fcc9bd2.png)\n\nExpressed in component form, our vector is $\\mathbf{a}=-2\\mathbf{i} + 4 \\mathbf{j}.$\n\nTo find the magnitude (length) of $\\mathbf{a},$ we can use the Pythagorean theorem on $\\triangle OXA.$\n\nThe legs of the triangle have lengths\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{OX}| & =|-2|=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{XA}| & =|\\,4\\,|=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the Pythagorean theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{|\\overset{\\to }{OX}{|}^{2}+|\\overset{\\to }{XA}{|}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186060911 1064 "We just saw that the magnitude of the vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,4 \\rangle$ was given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given a vector\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=a_x\\mathbf{i}+a_y\\,\\mathbf{j}=\\langle a_x,a_y \\rangle = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{a}_{x} \\\\ {a}_{y}\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\nthe magnitude of the vector can always be computed as\n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| = \\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**\n\nWe sometimes use the notation $|| \\mathbf a ||$ to denote the magnitude of a vector." 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186060911 1064 "We just saw that the magnitude of the vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,4 \\rangle$ was given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given a vector\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=a_x\\mathbf{i}+a_y\\,\\mathbf{j}=\\langle a_x,a_y \\rangle = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{a}_{x} \\\\ {a}_{y}\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\nthe magnitude of the vector can always be computed as\n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| = \\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**\n\nWe sometimes use the notation $|| \\mathbf a ||$ to denote the magnitude of a vector." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186065931 1078 #edb/ref 17592186065932 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186066352 1078 #edb/ref 17592186066357 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186067228 1078 #edb/ref 17592186067229 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186067234 1078 #edb/ref 17592186067237 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186067258 1078 #edb/ref 17592186067259 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186067876 1078 #edb/ref 17592186067878 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186067882 1078 #edb/ref 17592186067885 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186068236 1078 #edb/ref 17592186068241 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186069083 1074 "Use the slope formula: $$m=\\frac{3-5}{2-4}=\\frac{-2}{-2}=1.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186069195 1074 "The intercepts are $(0,4)$ and $(2,0)$. Hence $$m=\\frac{0-4}{2-0}=-2.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186069343 1074 "The plotted line is horizontal: its $y$-coordinate stays constant as $x$ changes. Its rise is zero, so its slope is $0$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186069344 1074 "The plotted line is vertical. Any two distinct points on it have zero horizontal change, so the slope would require division by zero. The slope is undefined." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186069348 1074 "The plotted points are $(1,0)$ and $(3,6)$. Thus $$m=\\frac{6-0}{3-1}=3.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186069451 1074 "The intercepts are $(-4,0)$ and $(0,2)$. Hence $$m=\\frac{2-0}{0-(-4)}=\\frac12.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186069452 1074 "The line falls as we move from left to right. Thus its rise is negative for a positive run, and its slope is negative." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186069511 1074 "The line rises as we move from left to right. Thus its rise and run have the same sign, and its slope is positive." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186070067 1078 #edb/ref 17592186070070 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186070073 1078 #edb/ref 17592186070078 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186070261 1075 #edb/ref 17592186730005 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186070261 1078 #edb/ref 17592186730005 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186070262 1075 #edb/ref 17592186730006 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186070262 1078 #edb/ref 17592186730006 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186070289 1078 #edb/ref 17592186070291 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186071270 1078 #edb/ref 17592186071275 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186073446 1074 "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n\n$$\n\\qquad\\:\\:\\: \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\color{red}2u+9v-6} &{\\color{red}{}=0} && {\\color{red}(1)} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2u+6v-8} &{\\color{blue}{}=0} && {\\color{blue}(2)}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $u$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+6v-8 &= 0 \\\\\n2u+6v &= 8 \\\\\n2u &= 8 - 6v \\\\\nu &= \\dfrac{8-6v}{2} \\\\\nu &= 4 - 3v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $u=4-3v$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $v\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2(4-3v)+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t8-6v+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n2+3v &= 0 \\\\\n3v &= -2 \\\\\nv &= -\\dfrac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $u\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u+9\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) -6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u-6-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u-12 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u &= 12 \\\\\n\tu &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=6$ and $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$, which can be written as $\\left(6,-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right).$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186073446 1074 "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n\n$$\\qquad\\:\\:\\:\n\\begin{cases}{\\color{red}2u+9v-6=0}\\hspace{2em}{\\color{red}(1)} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2u+6v-8=0}\\hspace{2em}{\\color{blue}(2)}\\end{cases}.$$\n\n\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $u$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+6v-8 &= 0 \\\\\n2u+6v &= 8 \\\\\n2u &= 8 - 6v \\\\\nu &= \\dfrac{8-6v}{2} \\\\\nu &= 4 - 3v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $u=4-3v$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $v\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2(4-3v)+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t8-6v+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n2+3v &= 0 \\\\\n3v &= -2 \\\\\nv &= -\\dfrac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $u\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u+9\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) -6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u-6-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u-12 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u &= 12 \\\\\n\tu &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=6$ and $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$, which can be written as $\\left(6,-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right).$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186073997 1074 "The first equation gives $q=3p-9$. Substitute into the second equation: $$5p+4(3p-9)=32\\implies17p=68\\implies p=4.$$ Hence $q=3(4)-9=3$. Substitution into both original equations gives $-9$ and $32$, as required." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186073998 1071 "Using the method of substitution, find the solution of\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-7a + 2b &=18 \\\\ -6a + 6b &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186073998 1071 "Using the method of substitution, find the solution of\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-7a + 2b &=18 \\\\ \\phantom{-}6a + 6b &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186073998 1074 "The second equation gives $b=-a$. Substitute into the first equation: $$-7a+2(-a)=18\\implies-9a=18\\implies a=-2.$$ Then $b=2$. Indeed, $-7(-2)+2(2)=18$ and $6(-2)+6(2)=0$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186074030 1074 "Substitute $y=x+5$ into the first equation: $$x+5(x+5)=13\\implies6x=-12\\implies x=-2.$$ Then $y=-2+5=3$. Both original equations hold, so the ordered solution is $(-2,3)$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186074047 1074 "From $5q+55=0$, we obtain $q=-11$. Substituting into the second equation gives $2p-33+3=0$, so $p=15$. Therefore $p-q=15-(-11)=26$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186074172 1074 "The second equation gives $b=2+4a$. Substitute into the first equation: $3a+2(2+4a)=4$, so $11a=0$. Thus $a=0$, $b=2$, and $a-b=-2$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186075033 1064 "Suppose that we want to solve, *without* using graphs, the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=4 \\\\ x-2 &=y\\,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order to solve the system without a graph, we will need to combine the equations into a single equation that can be solved using algebraic manipulation.\n\nOne way to combine the equations is called the **substitution method.** To use the substitution method, we substitute for one variable in terms of the other.\n\nFor instance, notice that we can make $y$ the subject of the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\color{red}y=x - 2\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression for $y$ into the first equation, we get an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =4 \\\\ x+(x-2) & =4 \\\\ 2x-2 & =4 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=3.$ But we also need to figure out what $y$ is. We can do this by substituting $x=3$ back into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-2 \\\\ y & =3-2 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 3$ and $y = 1$, which can be written as $\\left(3, 1\\right).$\n\n**Note:** Whenever we isolate the variable in one equation, we have to substitute it into the *other* equation. We cannot substitute a variable into the same equation it came from, because this will not give us any more information about the variable." 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186075033 1064 "Suppose that we want to solve, *without* using graphs, the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\\begin{cases}{\\color{blue}x+y=4} \\\\ {\\color{red}x-2=y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\end{cases}.$$\n\nIn order to solve the system without a graph, we will need to combine the equations into a single equation that can be solved using algebraic manipulation.\n\nOne way to combine the equations is called the **substitution method.** To use the substitution method, we substitute for one variable in terms of the other.\n\nFor instance, notice that we can make $y$ the subject of the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\color{red}y=x - 2\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression for $y$ into the first equation, we get an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}x+y} & ={\\color{blue}4} \\\\ {\\color{blue}x}+{\\color{red}(x-2)} & ={\\color{blue}4} \\\\ 2x-2 & =4 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=3.$ But we also need to figure out what $y$ is. We can do this by substituting $x=3$ back into the second equation:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\color{red}y} & ={\\color{red}x-2} \\\\ y & =3-2 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 3$ and $y = 1$, which can be written as $\\left(3, 1\\right).$\n\n**Note:** Whenever we isolate the variable in one equation, we have to substitute it into the *other* equation. We cannot substitute a variable into the same equation it came from, because this will not give us any more information about the variable." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186084842 1078 #edb/ref 17592186084845 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186084848 1078 #edb/ref 17592186084853 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186085884 1078 #edb/ref 17592186085886 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186085902 1078 #edb/ref 17592186085906 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186086406 1078 #edb/ref 17592186086408 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186086526 1078 #edb/ref 17592186086527 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186088018 1071 "![](images/33/330409f3fa32ee8701066cd3b434800a9cf3ed18639ae6f871494240d8237d7f.png)\n\nThe line segment $XY^(―)$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(2, 2)$ and $(-4, 2)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186088018 1071 "![](images/33/330409f3fa32ee8701066cd3b434800a9cf3ed18639ae6f871494240d8237d7f.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(2, 2)$ and $(-4, 2)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186088018 1074 "The center is the midpoint of the diameter: $((-4+2)/2,(2+2)/2)=(-1,2)$. The diameter has length $6$, so the radius is $3$. Thus $$(x+1)^2+(y-2)^2=9.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186088019 1071 "![](images/b2/b28b10cb5d033e2559f7e54e0745285f554d0bfbffa8e14d7bb6c2ba077fced8.png)\n\nThe line segment $XY^(―)$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(6, 4)$ and $(-4, 2)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186088019 1071 "![](images/b2/b28b10cb5d033e2559f7e54e0745285f554d0bfbffa8e14d7bb6c2ba077fced8.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(6, 4)$ and $(-4, 2)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186088019 1074 "The center is the midpoint of $(6,4)$ and $(-4,2)$, namely $(1,3)$. The squared diameter is $10^2+2^2=104$, so the squared radius is $104/4=26$. Hence $$(x-1)^2+(y-3)^2=26.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186088205 1074 "The diagram gives center $(1,3)$ and radius $3$. Substitute into $(x-h)^2+(y-k)^2=r^2$ to obtain $$(x-1)^2+(y-3)^2=9.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186088208 1074 "The diagram gives center $(-2,5)$ and radius $7$. Therefore $$(x+2)^2+(y-5)^2=49.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186088292 1074 "The squared radius is the squared distance from $C(-1,2)$ to $X(2,3)$: $r^2=(2+1)^2+(3-2)^2=10$. Hence the circle is $$(x+1)^2+(y-2)^2=10.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186088312 1074 "Compare $(x+5)^2+(y+1)^2=4$ with $(x-h)^2+(y-k)^2=r^2$. The center is $(-5,-1)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt4=2$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186089609 1078 #edb/ref 17592186089613 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186089615 1078 #edb/ref 17592186089620 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186089753 1078 #edb/ref 17592186089755 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186090418 1078 #edb/ref 17592186090421 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186090424 1078 #edb/ref 17592186090429 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186090430 1078 #edb/ref 17592186090435 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186090436 1078 #edb/ref 17592186090439 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186091156 1078 #edb/ref 17592186091160 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186091336 1078 #edb/ref 17592186091341 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186091342 1078 #edb/ref 17592186091343 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186091348 1078 #edb/ref 17592186091353 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186092571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089597 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186092573 1074 "For a parametric curve, $$L=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{(x')^2+(y')^2}\\,dt.$$ Here $x'=4\\cos4t$ and $y'=-4\\sin t$. Therefore $$L=4\\int_0^2\\sqrt{\\cos^2(4t)+\\sin^2t}\\,dt.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186092574 1074 "Differentiate: $x'=2t$ and $y'=3t^2+2$. Thus $$(x')^2+(y')^2=4t^2+(3t^2+2)^2=9t^4+16t^2+4.$$ Therefore $$L=\\int_{-1}^1\\sqrt{9t^4+16t^2+4}\\,dt.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186092597 1074 "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$. With $r=1$ and $\\theta=5\\pi/3$, the coordinates are $(\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2)$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186092703 1074 "The radius of $A$ is $r=\\sqrt{10+10}=2\\sqrt5$. Thus $B$ has radius $2r=4\\sqrt5$ and angle $\\pi/3$, giving $$B=(4\\sqrt5\\cos(\\pi/3),4\\sqrt5\\sin(\\pi/3))=(2\\sqrt5,2\\sqrt{15}).$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186092704 1074 "The coordinates of $A$ give $\\cos\\theta=\\sqrt2/2$ and $\\sin\\theta=-\\sqrt2/2$, so $\\theta=-\\pi/4$ modulo $2\\pi$. The angle of $B$ is $-\\pi/2$ modulo $2\\pi$, and its radius is $1$. Therefore $B=(0,-1)$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186092705 1074 "The diagram puts $B$ counterclockwise from $A$ in the second quadrant. Its angle is $\\pi/4+5\\pi/12=2\\pi/3$ and its radius is $8$. Therefore $$B=(8\\cos(2\\pi/3),8\\sin(2\\pi/3))=(-4,4\\sqrt3).$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186092706 1074 "The diagram puts $B$ clockwise from $A$ in the second quadrant. Its angle is $210^\\circ-75^\\circ=135^\\circ$ and its radius is $6$. Thus $$B=(6\\cos135^\\circ,6\\sin135^\\circ)=(-3\\sqrt2,3\\sqrt2).$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186092826 1074 "Differentiate: $x'=2t+2$, $y'=2t^2-2$. Their squared sum is $$4(t+1)^2+4(t^2-1)^2=4(t^4-t^2+2t+2).$$ Hence $$L=2\\int_{-3}^2\\sqrt{t^4-t^2+2t+2}\\,dt.$$ The square root keeps the speed nonnegative on the entire interval, including where $t+1$ changes sign." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186092856 1074 "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$. With $r=4$ and $\\theta=\\pi/4$, both coordinates equal $4\\sqrt2/2=2\\sqrt2$. Thus $P=(2\\sqrt2,2\\sqrt2)$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186092857 1074 "With $r=8$ and $\\theta=240^\\circ$, $$P=(8\\cos240^\\circ,8\\sin240^\\circ)=(-4,-4\\sqrt3).$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186092858 1074 "With $r=5$ and $\\theta=330^\\circ$, $$P=(5\\cos330^\\circ,5\\sin330^\\circ)=(\\tfrac{5\\sqrt3}2,-\\tfrac52).$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186093782 1078 #edb/ref 17592186093783 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186094153 1078 #edb/ref 17592186094154 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186094159 1078 #edb/ref 17592186094164 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186094165 1078 #edb/ref 17592186094166 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186096968 1071 "![](images/a8/a89a9c2ff1cfd53a9d9e33c60591b482cbfa4044e313b2d2c031046bb21e5ce3.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $3\\mathbf{a}$ and $-\\mathbf{a}$?" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186096968 1071 "![](images/a8/a89a9c2ff1cfd53a9d9e33c60591b482cbfa4044e313b2d2c031046bb21e5ce3.png)\n\nThe vector $a$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $3a$ and $-a$?" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186096968 1074 "Multiplying by $3$ preserves direction and triples length. Multiplying by $-1$ reverses direction and preserves length. The correct diagram therefore has a long vector pointing northeast labeled $3a$, and a vector one-third as long pointing southwest labeled $-a$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186097030 1071 "Given that $4\\mathbf{a}-12\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b})$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186097030 1071 "Given that $4a - 12b$ and $λ(a - 3b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186097030 1074 "Factor the first vector: $4a-12b=4(a-3b)$. Since this common vector is nonzero, equality with $\\lambda(a-3b)$ implies $\\lambda=4$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186097031 1071 "Given that $\\lambda (-0.5\\mathbf{a}+6\\mathbf{b})$ and $\\mathbf{a}-12\\mathbf{b}$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186097031 1071 "Given that $λ(-0.5a + 6b)$ and $a - 12b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186097031 1074 "We have $a-12b=-2(-0.5a+6b)$. Since the vectors are nonzero, equality requires $\\lambda=-2$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186097032 1071 "![](images/f5/f54e51921dfd2fb1b93bccec89553e174e2527866352626a5bf0846956b096bb.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the vector $\\mathbf{a}$. Which of the following diagrams shows the vector $-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}$?" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186097032 1071 "![](images/f5/f54e51921dfd2fb1b93bccec89553e174e2527866352626a5bf0846956b096bb.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the vector $a$. Which of the following diagrams shows the vector $-\\frac{1}{2}a$?" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186097032 1074 "The vector $-\\tfrac12a$ has half the length of $a$ and the opposite direction. The original vector points northeast, so the correct diagram shows a vector half as long pointing southwest." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186097247 107