88 1074 "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 16\\cdot 21\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{56}^{∘} \\\\  & =168\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{56}^{∘} \\\\  & =139.278… \\\\  & ≈139{\\text{ in}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of  $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =139-90 \\\\  & =49{\\text{ in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186106088 1074 "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 16\\cdot 21\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{56}^{∘} \\\\  & =168\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{56}^{∘} \\\\  & =139.278… \\\\  & \\approx 139{\\text{ in}}^{2},\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of  $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}BCD} & ={\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}ABD}-{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =139-90 \\\\  & =49{\\text{ in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186106089 1074 "The large triangle $ABD$ has area $\\tfrac12(12)(9)\\sin117^\\circ\\approx48.11435\\,\\mathrm{in}^2$. The concave quadrilateral is the part remaining after removing $\\triangle BCD$, so $$[BCD]=[ABD]-[ABCD]\\approx48.11435-15=33.11435\\,\\mathrm{in}^2.$$ Rounded to the nearest integer, the answer is $33\\,\\mathrm{in}^2$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186106094 1071 "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨k,1,1⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨5,0,4⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨6,2,5⟩$ are coplanar." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186106094 1071 "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a = ⟨k, 1, 1⟩$, $b = ⟨5, 0, 4⟩$, and $c = ⟨6, 2, 5⟩$ are coplanar." 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186106094 1074 "Coplanarity means the scalar triple product is zero. Since $b\\times c=\\langle-8,-1,10\\rangle$, we obtain $$a\\cdot(b\\times c)=-8k-1+10=9-8k=0.$$ Hence $k=\\tfrac98$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186106095 1071 "Find $t$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{p}=⟨3,t,t⟩$, $\\mathbf{q}=⟨1,3,1⟩$, and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨1,1,3⟩$ are coplanar." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186106095 1071 "Find $t$ given that the vectors $p=⟨3,t,t⟩$, $q=⟨1,3,1⟩$, and $r=⟨1,1,3⟩$ are coplanar." 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186106309 1071 "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{x}=⟨3,5,0⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨3,2,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨3,4,5⟩.$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186106309 1071 "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $x=⟨3,5,0⟩$, $y=⟨3,2,0⟩$ and $z=⟨3,4,5⟩.$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186106310 1071 "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-3,0,-2⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,5,-3⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨2,-1,4⟩$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186106310 1071 "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $a = ⟨ - 3, 0,-2⟩$, $b = ⟨1, 5,-3⟩$, and $c = ⟨2,-1, 4⟩$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186106310 1074 "The volume is the absolute value of the scalar triple product. Here $b\\times c=\\langle17,-10,-11\\rangle$, so $$V=|a\\cdot(b\\times c)|=|-3(17)-2(-11)|=|-29|=29.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186106347 1074 "The area formula gives $$\\tfrac12(5)(6)\\sin A=\\tfrac{15\\sqrt3}{2}\\implies\\sin A=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}.$$ The marked angle is acute in the given diagram, selecting $A=60^\\circ$ rather than its obtuse supplement." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186106439 1074 "Split the convex quadrilateral along $BD$ into triangles with included angles $80^\\circ$ and $50^\\circ$. Add