he following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $\\ge $ sign, the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ is part of the domain. Therefore, we draw a solid curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and shade the area *outside* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/ba/ba31bbc16c58860a4bc3c4d33ea1a61cb3d86d0e73d8aa6a67869b3e96a7fe32.png)" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186124800 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $\\ge $ sign, the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ is part of the domain. Therefore, we draw a solid curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and shade the area *outside* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/ba/ba31bbc16c58860a4bc3c4d33ea1a61cb3d86d0e73d8aa6a67869b3e96a7fe32.png)" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186124802 1074 "The logarithm requires $x+2>0$, hence $x>-2$. The denominator requires $y-2\\ne0$, hence $y\\ne2$. The correct diagram shades the half-plane to the right of $x=-2$, excludes that boundary, and removes the horizontal line $y=2$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186124803 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121415 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186124943 1071 "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F},$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186124943 1071 "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=-y\\,i+(3-x)\\,j.$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186124943 1074 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3-x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (-y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-xy+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3-x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+{g}^{′}(y) & =3-x \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =3 \\\\ g(y) & =3y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=3y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=-xy+3y+C.\n$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186124943 1074 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3-x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (-y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-xy+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3-x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+{g}^{′}(y) & =3-x \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =3 \\\\ g(y) & =3y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=3y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=-xy+3y+C.\n$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186124955 1071 "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)={e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186124955 1071 "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{-t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186124955 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{t}$ and $y={e}^{-t}$ into the above to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{e}^{-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{e}^{-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{e}^{-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{e}^{-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-2{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{-t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{