{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{12x}{y-1}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}}{(t+1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{6}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=4{t}^{2}+1,$ $\\text{d}u=8t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{9}^{25}\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{1/2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={u}^{3/2}{|}_{9}^{25} \\\\  & ={25}^{3/2}-{9}^{3/2} \\\\  & =125-27 \\\\  & =98\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186134512 1071 "Evaluate the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)4yz\\cos xds,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}\\hspace{0.167em}$ for $0 < t < \\frac{π}{2}$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186134512 1071 "Evaluate the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)4yz\\cos xds,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t) = ti + \\sin tj + k$ for $0 < t < \\frac{π}{2}$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186134512 1074 "Here $x=t$, $y=\\sin t$, and $z=1$. The speed is $\\sqrt{1+\\cos^2t}$. Thus $$\\int_C4yz\\cos x\\,ds=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}4\\sin t\\cos t\\sqrt{1+\\cos^2t}\\,dt.$$ Set $u=1+\\cos^2t$, so $du=-2\\sin t\\cos t\\,dt$. The integral becomes $$2\\int_1^2u^{1/2}\\,du=\\frac43(2\\sqrt2-1).$$ The omitted endpoints have finite limits and do not change this value." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186134513 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)(x^{3} - y)ds,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}$ for $-1 \\le t \\le 2$?" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186134513 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)(x^{3} - y)ds,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t) = 2ti + (2 - t)j$ for $-1 \\le t \\le 2$?" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186134513 1074 "Substitute $x=2t$, $y=2-t$ to get $x^3-y=8t^3+t-2$. The speed is $\\sqrt{2^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt5$. Therefore $$\\int_C(x^3-y)\\,ds=\\sqrt5\\int_{-1}^{2}(8t^3+t-2)\\,dt.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186135075 1064 "We wish to derive the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s =\\int_{a}^{b} f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we proceed in four steps, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We break the integration path $C$ into a series of line segments.\n\nWe choose a finite number of points $P_0,$ $P_1,$ $\\ldots,$ $P_n$ along $C$ and form the polygonal path\n\n$$\n\\overline{P_0P_1} \\cup \\overline{P_1P_2} \\cup \\cdots \\cup \\overline{P_{n-1}P_n}.\n$$\n\nOur polygonal path is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/2d/2d320e6a0f63a823e049203203cbc13c210e80bc98aab5a53bcb4e5299240920.png)\n\n**Step 2**: For each line segment, we approximate the area enclosed by $f(x,y)$ and $C$ between the two endpoints of the segment. To do this, we calculate the area of a rectangle formed by the segment and the surface $z=f(x,y).$\n\nThe width of each line segment is given by\n\n$$\n||\\overline{P_{k-1}P_k}|| .\n$$\n\nIf $P_k$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t_k),$ then by the mean value theorem,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\overset{―}{{P}_{k-1}{P}_{k}}‖ & =‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}({c}_{k})‖({t}_{k}-{t}_{k-1}) \\\\  & =‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}({c}_{k})‖\\mathrm{Δ}{t}_{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor some intermediate point $c_k \\in [t_{k-1}, t_k],$ where $\\Delta t_k = t_k - t_{k-1}.$\n\nTherefore, the area formed by the rectangle with a width of $\\big\\| \\overline{P_{k-1}P_k} \\big\\|$ and a height of $f(c_k)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(c_k) \\| \\mathbf r'(c_k)\\|\\Delta t_k.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We sum the areas of all of our small rectangles, which gives us an *approximation* of our line integral. We get\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s \\approx \\sum_{k=1}^{n} f(\\mathbf r(c_k)) \\| \\mathbf{r}'(c_k) \\| \\Delta t_k.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We take the limit as the width of each rectangle tends to zero (or equivalently, as the number of rectangles tends to infinity). This gives us the exact representation of our line integral:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty }\\sum_{k=1}^{n} f(\\mathbf r(c_k)) \\| \\mathbf{r}'(c_k) \\| \\Delta t_k = \\int_{a}^{b} f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186135075 1064 "We wish to derive the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s =\\int_{a}^{b} f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we proceed in four steps, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We break the integration path $C$ into a series of line segments.\n\nWe choose a finite number of points $P_0,$ $P_1,$ $\\ldots,$ $P_n$ along $C$ and form the polygonal path\n\n$$\n\\overline{P_0