\pi].$ \n\n![](images/4b/4b2ab0c3ef67935283cb248f645e71f4b8919cbda78dc263ff2402fc161849d5.png)\n\nWe wish to calculate the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\textrm dt.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},$ along the curve $C$ we have that \n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad y = \\sin{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))=y\\,i+x\\,j=\\operatorname{sin}t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute $\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)$ by taking the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{'}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}t+{cos}^{2}t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}t-{sin}^{2}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{'}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}2t|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}2\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}(0)) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186135124 1064 "Suppose that $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,  \\mathbf i + x \\, \\mathbf j$ and that $C$ is the path along the semi-circle $\\mathbf r(t) = \\cos t \\,\\mathbf i + \\sin t \\,\\mathbf j$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi].$ \n\n![](images/4b/4b2ab0c3ef67935283cb248f645e71f4b8919cbda78dc263ff2402fc161849d5.png)\n\nWe wish to calculate the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\textrm dt.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},$ along the curve $C$ we have that \n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad y = \\sin{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) &=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThen, we compute $\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)$ by taking the dot product:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}2t\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}2t|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}2\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}(0)) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186135126 1064 "Here, we derive the following result in the two-dimensional case.\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\textrm dt\n$$\n\nLet $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ be a continuous vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ We wish to compute the work done by $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a smooth curve $C.$\n\nSuppose that $C$ can be parameterized by the function $\\mathbf r(t),$ where $t \\in [a,b].$ We divide the interval $[a,b]$ into subintervals $[t_{i-1},t_i]$ of equal length. This divides $C$ into subarcs, and each subinterval $[t_{i-1},t_i]$ traces out a subarc from the point $P_{i-1}$ to the point $P_i$ along the curve. Let the length of the subarc traced out over $[t_{i-1}, t_i]$ be equal to $\\Delta s_i.$ \n\nWe now pick a point $\\widetilde t_i \\in [t_{i-1}, t_i],$ and let $\\widetilde{P}(\\widetilde{x}, \\widetilde{y})$ be the point on $C$ corresponding to $\\widetilde{t}_i,$ \n\n![](images/ce/ced3b31a828cb4b7e04c8ab772445e704be8b234571480e25f676b3f7f3551bb.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\Delta s_i$ is small. As the particle moves from $P_{i-1}$ to $P_i$ along the curve, its direction of travel is approximately $\\mathbf T(\\widetilde x_i, \\widetilde y_i),$ the unit tangent vector at $\\widetilde P_i.$ Thus, the work done by $\\mathbf F$ in moving the particle from $P_{i-1}$ to $P_i$ is approximately equal to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F (\\widetilde x_i, \\widetilde y_i) \\cdot [\\Delta s_i \\,\\mathbf T(\\widetilde x_i, \\widetilde y_i)] = [\\mathbf F(\\widetilde x_i, \\widetilde y_i) \\cdot \\mathbf T(\\widetilde x_i, \\widetilde y_i)]\\Delta s_i.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total work done by $\\mathbf F$ in moving the particle from the initial point to the terminal point along $C$ is approximately equal to\n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} [\\mathbf F(\\widetilde x_i, \\widetilde y_i) \\cdot \\mathbf T(\\widetilde x_i, \\widetilde y_i)] \\Delta s_i.\n$$\n\nThese approximations become better as the number of subintervals $n$ increases. Therefore, we define the work done by $\\mathbf F$ as the limit of the Riemann sum above, that is\n\n$$\nW = \\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf T \\,\\textrm ds.\n$$\n\nThis equation says that work done by $\\mathbf F$ is the line integral with respect to arc length along $C$ of the tangential component of the force.\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- The unit tangent vector can be expressed in terms of $\\mathbf r(t)$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{|| \\mathbf r'(t) ||}.\n$$\n\n- The arc-length element $\\textrm d s$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = ||\\mathbf r'(t) || \\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $W,$ we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{C}F\\cdot T\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(F(r(t))\\cdot \\frac{{r}^{'}(t)}{||{r}^{'}(t)||})||{r}^{'}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{'}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\textrm dt.\n$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186135126 1064 "Here, we derive the following result in the two-dimensional case.\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\textrm dt\n$$\n\nLet $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ be a continuous vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ We wish to compute the work done by $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a smooth curve $C.$\n\nSuppose that $C$ can be parameterized by th