expressed in terms of $\\mathbf r(t)$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{|| \\mathbf r'(t) ||}.\n$$\n\n- The arc-length element $\\textrm d s$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = ||\\mathbf r'(t) || \\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $W,$ we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{T}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot \\frac{{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||})||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\textrm dt.\n$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186149081 1078 #edb/ref 17592186149086 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186149087 1078 #edb/ref 17592186149090 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186149099 1078 #edb/ref 17592186149103 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186152425 1074 "Acceleration is $a(t)=v'(t)=4-6t$. At $t=2$, $a=-8\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}$. Newton's second law gives $F=2(-8)=-16\\,\\mathrm N$, so the requested magnitude is $16\\,\\mathrm N$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186152426 1074 "Acceleration is $v'(t)=3t^2-2e^t$. Multiply by the mass $5\\,\\mathrm{kg}$: $$F(t)=5(3t^2-2e^t)=15t^2-10e^t,$$ measured in newtons." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186152427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149093 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186152428 1074 "Differentiate position twice: $s'(t)=t^{-1/2}$ and $s''(t)=-\\tfrac12t^{-3/2}$. Thus $F(t)=8s''(t)=-4t^{-3/2}\\,\\mathrm N$. At $t=4$, the force is $-1/2\\,\\mathrm N$, whose magnitude is $1/2\\,\\mathrm N$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186159158 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159163 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186159170 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159172 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186159176 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159177 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186159182 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159186 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186161896 1071 "Consider the curves $C_1$ and $C_2,$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{3},\\,t⟩, & 0\\le t\\le 3 \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨3{u}^{3},\\,3u⟩, & 0\\le u\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k\n$$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = 3k\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k\n$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186161896 1071 "Consider the curves $C_1$ and $C_2,$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩, & 0\\le t\\le 3 \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(u) & =⟨3{u}^{3},\\hspace{0.222em}3u⟩, & 0\\le u\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k\n$$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = 3k\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k\n$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186162326 1071 "Suppose $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C_{1}}f(x, y)ds = 2\n$$ for some function $f(x, y)$, where $C_{1}$ is parametrized by $r_{1}(t) = ⟨t, t^{2}⟩$ for $0 \\le t \\le 1$.\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-C_{1}}f(x, y)ds =-2\n$$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-C_{1}}f(x, y)ds = 2\n$$\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C_{2}}f(x, y)ds = 4,\n$$ where $C_{2}$ is parametrized by $r_{2}(u) = ⟨2u, 4u^{2}⟩$ for $0 \\le u \\le \\frac{1}{2}$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186162326 1071 "Suppose $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C_{1}}f(x, y)ds = 2\n$$ for some function $f(x, y)$, where $C_{1}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.222em}{t}^{2}⟩$ for $0 \\le t \\le 1$.\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-C_{1}}f(x, y)ds =-2\n$$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-C_{1}}f(x, y)ds = 2\n$$\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C_{2}}f(x, y)ds = 4,\n$$ where $C_{2}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(u)=⟨2u,\\hspace{0.222em}4{u}^{2}⟩$ for $0 \\le u \\le \\frac{1}{2}$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186162326 1074 "A scalar line integral with respect to arc length is unchanged by reversing orientation, so statement II is true and statement I is false. Also $r_2(u)=r_1(2u)$ for $0\\le u\\le\\tfrac12$, which traverses the same curve once. Its scalar line integral is $2$, rather than $4$. Thus only II must be true." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186162328 1074 "The integral of $1$ with respect to arc length is the length of $C$. The diagram shows a quarter-circle arc of radius $2$ and its straight chord. The arc length is $2(\\pi/2)=\\pi$, and the chord length is $\\sqrt{2^2+2^2}=2\\sqrt2$. Hence $$\\int_C ds=\\pi+2\\sqrt2.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186162329 1074 "The integral of $1$ with respect to arc length is the length of the curve. A semicircle of radius $3$ has length half of $2\\pi(3)$, so $$\\int_C ds=3\\pi.$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186162330 1074 "Scalar line integrals are independent of orientation, so I holds. Set $t=2\\theta$; as $\\theta$ runs from $0$ to $\\pi$, $t$ runs from $0$ to $2\\pi$. Both parametrizations therefore traverse the same circle once, giving the same integral $k$. Thus II holds. III does not hold for arbitrary $k$, so the statements that must be true are I and II." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186163624 1078 #edb/ref 17592186163627 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186163630 1078 #edb/ref 17592186163634 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186163636 1078 #edb/ref 17592186163641 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186163648 1078 #edb/ref 17592186163651 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186167091 1074 "The diagram is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$. Differentiating gives $2x+2yy'=0$, so $$\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}$$ for $y\\ne0$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186167092 1074 "The diagram gives center $(1,-1)$ and radius $3$, so $(x-1)^2+(y+1)^2=9$. Differentiating gives $2(x-1)+2(y+1)y'=0$. Therefore $$\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x-1}{y+1}$$ for $y\\ne-1$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186167093 1074 "The ellipse has horizontal and vertical semiaxes $3$ and $2$, so its equation is $x^2/9+y^2/4=1$. Differentiating yields $2x/9+yy'/2=0$, hence $$\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{4x}{9y}$$ for $y\\ne0$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186167095 1074 "The right-opening parabola has vertex $(0,0)$ and passes through $(1,2)$. Its equation is $y^2=4x$. Differentiate to obtain $2yy'=4$, so $$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac2y$$ for $y\\ne0$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186173404 1078 #edb/ref 17592186173409 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186173410 1078 #edb/ref 17592186173414 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186173422 1078 #edb/ref 17592186173426 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186174658 1078 #edb/ref 17592186174662 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186175820 1074 "Recall that the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x,y \\rangle = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 4\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\frac{16{sin}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}2\\operatorname{cos}t},\\hspace{0.222em}4\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\cos{t}}.$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186175820 1074 "Recall that the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x,y \\rangle = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 4\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\frac{16{sin}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\cos{t}}.$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186175822 1074 "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(x,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}2{x}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ and $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} 2x^2}.$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186175822 1074 "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,2{x}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ and $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} 2x^2}.$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186175823 1074 "Recall that the hyperbola $\\dfrac{z^2}{4} - \\dfrac{x^2}{16} = 1$ for $z \\geq 2$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x , z \\rangle = \\big \\langle 4\\tan{t}, \\, 2\\sec{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\n\\qquad t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{sec}^{2}⁡t}{4}-\\frac{16{tan}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{tan}t,\\hspace{0.167em}y,\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}2\\operatorname{sec}t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$ and $y \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\sec{t}}.$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186175823 1074 "Recall that the hyperbola $\\dfrac{z^2}{4} - \\dfrac{x^2}{16} = 1$ for $z \\geq 2$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x , z \\rangle = \\big \\langle 4\\tan{t}, \\, 2\\sec{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\n\\qquad t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{sec}^{2}⁡t}{4}-\\frac{16{tan}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y,\\,2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$ and $y \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\sec{t}}.$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186176191 1071 "$\\mathbf{f}(t, z) = ⟨2\\cos t, \\underline{\\phantom{xxxx}}, z⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$. What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$?" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186176191 1071 "$f(t, z) = ⟨2\\cos t, _____, z⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t, z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$. What is the second component of $f$?" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186176191 1074 "We require $x^2+y^2=4$ with $x=2\\cos t$. The choice $y=2\\sin t$ gives $$x^2+y^2=4\\cos^2t+4\\sin^2t=4.$$ It parametrizes the full circular cross-section while $z$ is free. It is the only listed component with this property." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186176192 1071 "$\\mathbf{f}(t, y) = ⟨2\\cos t, y, \\underline{\\phantom{xxxx}}⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,y)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $9x^{2} + 4z^{2} = 36$. What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$?" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186176192 1071 "$f(t, y) = ⟨2\\cos t, y, _____⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t, y)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $9x^{2} + 4z^{2} = 36$. What is the third component of $f$?" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186176192 1074 "Substitute $x=2\\cos t$ into $9x^2+4z^2=36$. Choosing $z=3\\sin t$ gives $$9(2\\cos t)^2+4(3\\sin t)^2=36.$$ The parameter $y$ is free, as required for the cylinder." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186176193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173416 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186176193 1071 "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,z)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{___},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(x,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $y=4{x}^{2}.$ What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186176193 1071 "$$\nf(x,z)=⟨x,\\,\\text{___},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $y=4{x}^{2}.$ What is the second component of $f?$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186176193 1074 "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}4{x}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}4{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186176193 1074 "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,4{x}^{2},\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $4{x}^{2}.$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186176194 1071 "$\\mathbf{f}(x, y) = ⟨\\underline{\\phantom{xxxx}}, y, 9y^{2}⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(x, y)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $9y^{2} = z$. What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$?" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186176194 1071 "$f(x, y) = ⟨___, y, 9y^{2}⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x, z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $9y^{2} = z$. What is the first component of $f$?" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186176194 1074 "The equation $z=9y^2$ restricts $y$ and $z$, while $x$ is free. Thus $f(x,y)=\\langle x,y,9y^2\\rangle$, and its first component is $x$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186176195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173428 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186176195 1071 "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,z)=⟨3\\operatorname{sec}t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{_____},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩,\\hspace{2em}t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,z)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=9$ for $x\\ge 3.$ What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186176195 1071 "$$\nf(t,z)=⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\text{_____},\\,z⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=9$ for $x\\ge 3.$ What is the second component of $f?$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186176195 1074 "Recall that the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=9$ for $x\\ge 3$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-(3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =9({sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sec}t,\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}3\\operatorname{tan}t},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}3\\operatorname{tan}t}.$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186176195 1074 "Recall that the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=9$ for $x\\ge 3$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-(3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =9({sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186176400 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{f}(x, t) = ⟨x, \\underline{\\phantom{xxxx}}, 2\\sec t⟩, t ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(x,t)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $3z^{2} - 4y^{2} = 12$ for $z \\ge 2$. What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$?" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186176400 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle f(x, t) = ⟨x, _____, 2\\sec t⟩, t ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x, t)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $3z^{2} - 4y^{2} = 12$ for $z \\ge 2$. What is the second component of $f$?" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186176400 1074 "Set $y=\\sqrt3\\tan t$ and $z=2\\sec t$. Then $$3z^2-4y^2=12\\sec^2t-12\\tan^2t=12.$$ On $-\\pi/2<t<\\pi/2$, $\\sec t\\ge1$, so $z\\ge2$. Thus the required second component is $\\sqrt3\\tan t$." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186176984 1064 "In this lesson, we will explore strategies for parametrizing cylinders. \n\nParametrizing a cylinder is no more difficult than parametrizing its base curve. The only difference is that we need a *second* parameter to control the variation parallel to the cylinder's axis. This second parameter is usually allowed to vary arbitrarily. \n\nFor example, consider the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\ny = 8x^2.\n$$\n\nNotice that $y = y(x)$ is an explicit function of $x.$ Therefore, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on this surface, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}8{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! We have successfully found a parametrization of this cylinder. \n\nNote that, under this parametrization\n\n- the parameter $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ controls the movement along the base curve, and\n\n- the parameter $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ controls the movement parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nLet's see some more examples." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186176984 1064 "In this lesson, we will explore strategies for parametrizing cylinders. \n\nParametrizing a cylinder is no more difficult than parametrizing its base curve. The only difference is that we need a *second* parameter to control the variation parallel to the cylinder's axis. This second parameter is usually allowed to vary arbitrarily. \n\nFor example, consider the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\ny = 8x^2.\n$$\n\nNotice that $y = y(x)$ is an explicit function of $x.$ Therefore, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on this surface, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,8{x}^{2},\\,z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! We have successfully found a parametrization of this cylinder. \n\nNote that, under this parametrization\n\n- the parameter $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ controls the movement along the base curve, and\n\n- the parameter $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ controls the movement parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nLet's see some more examples." 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186177077 1064 "Let's find a parametrization of the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{25}+\\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1.\n$$\n\nThe base curve in the $xz$-plane is an ellipse centered at $O$ with semi-axes of lengths $\\sqrt{25} = 5$ and $\\sqrt{16} = 4.$ Therefore, we can parametrize the base curve in the $xz$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\langle x,z \\rangle = \\big\\langle 5\\cos{t}, \\: 4\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}4\\operatorname{sin}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nNote that the axis of the cylinder coincides with the $y$-axis. Therefore, the parameter $y$ is allowed to vary arbitrarily." 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186177077 1064 "Let's find a parametrization of the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{25}+\\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1.\n$$\n\nThe base curve in the $xz$-plane is an ellipse centered at $O$ with semi-axes of lengths $\\sqrt{25} = 5$ and $\\sqrt{16} = 4.$ Therefore, we can parametrize the base curve in the $xz$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\langle x,z \\rangle = \\big\\langle 5\\cos{t}, \\: 4\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nNote that the axis of the cylinder coincides with the $y$-axis. Therefore, the parameter $y$ is allowed to vary arbitrarily." 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186177078 1064 "To find a parametrization of one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{25}-\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1\n$$\n\nfor $x \\geq 5,$ recall that the corresponding hyperbola in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x,y \\rangle = \\big\\langle 5 \\sec{t}, \\: 4 \\tan{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\\qquad t\\in\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)\n$$\n\nWe can check that this indeed parametrizes our base hyperbola by substituting the parametric equations into the equation of the base curve: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{sec}t,\\hspace{0.222em}4\\operatorname{tan}t,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186177078 1064 "To find a parametrization of one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{25}-\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1\n$$\n\nfor $x \\geq 5,$ recall that the corresponding hyperbola in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x,y \\rangle = \\big\\langle 5 \\sec{t}, \\: 4 \\tan{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\\qquad t\\in\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)\n$$\n\nWe can check that this indeed parametrizes our base hyperbola by substituting the parametric equations into the equation of the base curve: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186179603 1074 "The closed curve $C$ is the unit circle. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))=-y\\,i+x\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186179603 1074 "The closed curve $C$ is the unit circle. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) &=-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186180021 1071 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186180021 1071 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+5\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 true] [17592186180021 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-25{sin}^{2}⁡t-25{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-25)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-25t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-25(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-50\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543279 17592186576411 false] [17592186180021 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ w