ction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(6\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}t)+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t \\\\  & =-16\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-16\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace